@startuml sequence-dataverse-sync
!include elvira.iuml
' =============================================================================
' What: the two-way sync is generic (apps/harvest): inbound and outbound both
'       run a three-way merge against the HarvestRecord's synced state, so a
'       push that triggers the remote's own webhook comes back as a no-op, and
'       deletions no webhook reports are found by the source's scheduled run;
'       Dataverse appears only as the connector and its two webhook views.
' Kind: UML sequence diagram, proposal (IP-034, accepted, unified 2026-11-16).
'       Scope: validate, publish inbound, outbound push, scheduled reconcile,
'       with Dataverse as the connector. Omitted: remote creation, retries.
' Source: IP-034 decisions (2026-11-16); Dataverse 6.x guides workflows and
'         native API (read 2026-11-16); apps/dataverse/views.py.
' =============================================================================

title Two-way sync with a writing connector (Dataverse): validate, publish, push, reconcile (IP-034, 2026-11-16)

actor "Librarian" as librarian
participant "Dataverse\n(intranet only)" as dv
participant "Webhook views\n(apps.dataverse)" as hooks
participant "Sync\n(apps.harvest)" as sync
participant "Dataverse\nconnector" as connector
database "Entry ·\nHarvestRecord" as db
queue "Celery" as celery

== librarian publishes a book dataset in Dataverse ==
librarian -> dv : Publish
dv -> hooks : PrePublishDataset http/sr\nPOST /api/v1/dataverse-validate
hooks -> connector : validate(record)
hooks --> dv : "OK" or "FAILED: invalid ISBN"\n(failure stops the publish)
dv -> dv : publish version
dv -[$accent_msg()]> hooks : <b>PostPublishDataset http/sr</b>\n<b>POST /api/v1/dataverse-prepublish</b>
hooks -> sync : apply_record(source, record_id)
sync -> connector : fetch → versions/:latest-published
sync -> db : load HarvestRecord.synced_record
sync -[$accent_msg()]> sync : <b>three-way merge per field: changed only remotely → take;</b>\n<b>both → later version wins, HarvestConflict</b>
sync -> db : save entry + synced_record
hooks --> dv : OK

== librarian edits the entry in Elvira ==
librarian -> db : PUT /api/v1/catalogs/{c}/entries/{id}
db -> celery : on commit: harvest.push(entry, source)\n(deduplicated per entry)
celery -> sync : push(entry, source)
sync -> connector : fetch (values, version)
sync -> sync : merge per field (same rules)
sync -> connector : push(record_id, merged, expected_version)
connector -> dv : PUT editMetadata (sourceLastUpdateTime)\nPOST publish?type=updatecurrent
sync -> db : synced_record = pushed state,\nnew versions
dv -> hooks : PostPublishDataset (caused by the push)
hooks -> sync : apply_record(source, record_id)
sync -> sync : nothing differs from synced_record → no write

== the source's schedule (e.g. every 15 minutes) ==
celery -> sync : harvest.run(source)
sync -> connector : records(cursor): changed since cursor,\ndeaccessioned or gone = deleted
sync -> db : changed → merge; deleted →\nEntry.withdrawn_at = now (loans keep running)

caption Only the webhook views and the connector know Dataverse; the merge, conflicts, push, reconcile and withdrawal are the generic sync of\napps/harvest and work the same for any connector that can write. The pre-publish check refuses bad book metadata before anything is\npublished; a push updates the current version in place and the webhook call it causes finds nothing new.
footer ELVIRA · FIIT STU · EvilFlowersCatalog · docs/diagrams/sequence-dataverse-sync.puml
@enduml
